<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!-- GENERATED by sim/gen_humanoid_v6.py . Do not hand-edit; edit the generator.

     humanoid_v6_atom.urdf : shoulder_v2 (3 DOF) + the real ATOM arm.

     v5 used shoulder_v1, which had pitch + yaw and NO abduction, and whose yaw
     was collision-limited to +/-10 deg. v2 replaces yaw with roll (abduction)
     about X and twist about the humerus axis, all three concurrent at C.

     JOINTS PER SIDE
       *_shoulder_pitch  Y through C   flexion, positive = FORWARD both sides
       *_shoulder_roll   X through C   abduction. 0 = arm UP (CAD neutral),
                                       1.5708 = straight out, 3.1416 = hanging
       *_shoulder_twist  Z, humerus    internal/external rotation
       *_elbow           unchanged from v5

     REST POSE IS roll = 3.1416, NOT ZERO. The CAD neutral is arm-straight-up
     and the mount plate geometry forbids inverting the module, so hanging is
     reached through roll. Anything driving this model must apply that offset.

     Module mass 771 g/side (integrated from mesh geometry by
     sim/shoulder_v2_prep.py) vs 352 g for v1. ATOM arm 586 g/side.

     MEASURED TRAVEL (swept against real collision geometry)
       pitch  +/-90 deg  - servo limited, structure clear to +/-95
       roll   0 .. 185 deg - full abduction. v1 could not abduct AT ALL.
       twist  +/-90 deg  - but COUPLED TO ROLL:
                roll 0..160  -> twist +/-90 (full)
                roll 170     -> twist +/-70
                roll 180     -> twist +/-25   (hanging; a bent elbow with
                                internal rotation sweeps across the chest)
       elbow  0 .. 120, contact-stopped at ~115, unchanged from v5.

     Roll and twist limits are left GENEROUS on purpose: the coupling above
     cannot be expressed as a box limit, and contact geometry is modelled, so
     the sim refuses the bad combinations rather than hiding them. A
     self-collision-aware controller is the proper fix.
-->
